# The fact examples' prelude: the assertion directives and the smallest schema a document can be
# captured against. Prepended to every document by CorpusDocuments.prelude(FACTS).
#
# A neighbour of the corpus folder and deliberately not part of it. A corpus document carries a
# classification verdict, renders into an author-facing page, and is read as a statement about what
# graphitron generates; a document here is about the shape of the store itself, which is a
# contributor's concern and no author's. Sharing the folder would put store plumbing on that page
# and make every fact example look like a claim about generated code. What the two share is the
# mechanism and nothing else: the same loader, the same directives, the same comparison.
#
# The prelude is minimal on purpose. The corpus prelude declares the classification enums and a
# CorpusAnchor bound to a table, because a verdict needs something to be a verdict about. A fact
# example needs a query root and no more, so what a document contains is what the example is about
# and a reader can hold the whole population in their head.
#
# A document named name.<file>.<epoch>.graphqls is one version of one file of a graph read in steps.
# The file is a short name that only tells the files apart; the epoch is the second that version is
# read at. The graph is read once per epoch any of its files names: the files with a version at it
# are written and dated at it, the graph is captured over every file it has met so far at that
# instant, and the assertions written at it are checked against what it leaves. A file the reading
# did not write keeps its bytes, so its rows keep an earlier reading's mark. A timestamp column is
# compared as epoch seconds, so a mark column reads as the reading that last wrote the row, which is
# how a document tells what a reading swept, what it kept and what it wrote again. The prelude is
# the stepped graph's own file, written at its first epoch.

directive @expectEquals(relation: String!, rows: String!) repeatable on SCHEMA
directive @assertContains(relation: String!, rows: String!) repeatable on SCHEMA
directive @assertAbsent(relation: String!, rows: String!) repeatable on SCHEMA
# The relation holds no row for this document's graph.
directive @assertEmpty(relation: String!) repeatable on SCHEMA

# The configuration a graph is captured under, as a build would have handed it to the run: read by
# the fact harness off the document's text before the capture, since a reading is told its
# configuration rather than finding it. A document without it is a run that declared nothing. The
# output coordinates travel together, all three or none; the schema files are always the document's
# own. In a graph read in steps, a reading takes it from the files as they then stand.
directive @withConfiguration(
  supergraph: String,
  tenantColumn: String,
  outputPackage: String,
  outputJooqPackage: String,
  outputDirectory: String,
  lintDisabledRules: [String!],
  lintExcludedTypes: [String!],
  mount: String,
  unmount: String
) on SCHEMA

type Query { ping: String }
